% The shared test framework, prepended by run-ps-test.sh to any test whose
% first line is `%!testlib`.
%
% It is OPT-IN, and it has to be. Several suites here assert that a
% program's own dictionary starts empty or count the dictionary stack, and
% a framework defined into userdict for them would be the thing they
% report. A test that asks for the framework accepts that its userdict
% carries these names; a test that does not ask is prepended nothing and
% runs exactly as it did before. Six suites also read their own source
% through currentfile, and prepending would shift what they read.
%
% WHY THIS EXISTS. Every one of these procedures was written out again in
% file after file -- /assert alone in a hundred and forty-six of them, in
% bodies differing only in whitespace, a comment, or which counter they
% increment. That is not merely repetition: it means a fault found in one
% copy is fixed in one copy. The graphics-state unwind below was added to a
% single file after a refused case there left a singular transform behind
% and five later cases raised undefinedresult, which that file's harness
% read as those cases correctly refusing the thing they were actually
% testing. One false pass, and a hundred and forty-five other files with
% the same shape and no reason to be looked at.
%
% So the rule this file exists to keep is: a case that may raise is run in
% ONE place, and that place puts the world back.

% The framework's own state, in a dictionary of its own rather than in
% userdict. A case that opens a dictionary and raises leaves it on the
% dictionary stack, and a def while it sits there writes into the case's
% leavings instead of here -- so the answers this framework has to keep
% across an unwind are kept somewhere an unwind cannot move.
/.testlib 8 dict def

% ---- the verdict
%
% A count rather than a flag, so a run says how much is wrong rather than
% that something is. In userdict, where a suite can read them.
/failcount 0 def
/checkcount 0 def

% bool (what)  assert  -
% Record one check. The message prints only on failure, because a suite
% that prints a line per success buries the one line that matters.
/assert {                               % bool (what)  .  -
    /checkcount checkcount 1 add store
    exch { pop }{
        (FAIL: ) print print (\n) print flush
        /failcount failcount 1 add store
    } ifelse
} bind def

% bool (name) (what)  assert2  -
% The same, for suites that name the subject and the question separately
% and print them together.
/assert2 {                              % bool (name) (what)  .  -
    /checkcount checkcount 1 add store
    3 -1 roll { pop pop }{
        (FAIL: ) print exch print ( ) print print (\n) print flush
        /failcount failcount 1 add store
    } ifelse
} bind def

% -  verdict  -
% Print the one line the harness reads. A suite that reaches here having
% checked nothing says so: a suite whose cases all failed to run is not a
% suite that passed, and reporting success for it is the failure mode this
% whole file is about.
/verdict {
    checkcount 0 eq {
        (FAILURES: this suite made no checks at all\n) print
    }{
        failcount 0 eq { (SUCCESS\n) print }
        { (FAILURES: ) print failcount 8 string cvs print (\n) print } ifelse
    } ifelse
    flush
} bind def

% ---- running a case that may raise
%
% Three things a raising case leaves behind, and all three are the NEXT
% case's problem rather than its own, because a case that raises never
% reaches its own cleanup:
%
%   the operand stack, holding whatever the case had built
%   the dictionary stack, holding any dictionary the case opened
%   the graphics state stack, holding any state the case saved
%
% The third does the real damage, because the state is not inert: a
% transform, a clip or a colour left in force silently becomes the
% environment every later case runs in, and a case failing for that reason
% looks exactly like a case failing for its own.
%
% The unwind goes all the way rather than by one level. A case may open
% more than it closed, and the next case is entitled to the world the
% suite started in -- which, for a test program with no save of its own
% outstanding, is what grestoreall restores. A suite deliberately holding
% an outer graphics state across its cases must therefore not use these.

% -  .testlib.unwind  -
% Put the operand and dictionary stacks back. Called after every case,
% whether it returned or raised, because leftovers on either belong to the
% case that made them and not to the next one.
%
% The operand stack is popped back to the depth the case STARTED at, not
% cleared. Clearing also throws away whatever the suite had on the stack
% below the case, which is not the case's to discard: a suite that records
% the depth and then checks a case left it unchanged is measuring this
% runner, and with a wholesale clear it can only ever measure zero. The
% depth is recorded in `base` by outcome, before the case runs.
%
% This needs no mark, which is the reason it is done by counting. A mark is
% visible to the case -- a body that reads `count`, or lays and clears a
% mark of its own, gets a different answer with one of ours underneath it,
% and suites here have bodies that do exactly that.
.testlib /unwind {
    count //.testlib /base get sub
    dup 0 gt { { pop } repeat }{ pop } ifelse
    countdictstack //.testlib /depth get sub
    dup 0 gt { { end } repeat }{ pop } ifelse
} bind put

% { body }  outcome  /ok | /errorname
% Run a case and say how it ended. The error NAME is the answer, not a
% boolean, because "it raised" and "it raised the right thing" are
% different questions, and a test that can only ask the first passes when
% the interpreter starts raising something else.
%
% THE GRAPHICS STATE IS PUT BACK ONLY IF THE CASE RAISED. Both halves of
% that matter.
%
% Restoring always is wrong. A suite may be a sequence rather than a set of
% independent cases: one case selects a font and the next shows text with
% it, and undoing the first because the framework tidies after every case
% breaks the second. MEASURED on two suites, four and three cases failing.
%
% Not restoring at all is the defect this framework was written for. A case
% that raises never reaches its own cleanup, so its half-finished graphics
% state -- a transform, a clip, a colour -- becomes the environment every
% later case runs in, and a case failing for that reason looks exactly like
% a case failing for its own.
%
% A case that returned meant what it did; a case that raised did not. So the
% state is captured before the case and put back only on the raising path.
%
% It is put back from the copy rather than by unwinding the graphics state
% stack. grestoreall is the obvious tool and it is wrong: it unwinds to the
% state saved by the most recent SAVE, and a job has one outstanding, so it
% resets to the state the job began with -- discarding whatever the suite
% set at its own top level. Restoring from a copy is also exact where a
% single grestore is not, since a case that opened two states and closed
% neither would be unwound only to its own inner one.
%
% What is not undone is the DEPTH: a case that leaks a gsave leaves the
% stack one deeper, and nothing here can tell that from a state the suite
% opened on purpose. The state the next case sees is right; the depth is
% not, and no operator here can measure it.
%
% The answer is parked in the framework's dictionary before the unwind,
% because the unwind clears the operand stack and the answer is on it.
%
% No mark is laid to find the case's leavings by, though that is the obvious
% way to tidy an operand stack. A mark is visible to the case: a body that
% reads `count`, or that lays and clears a mark of its own, gets a different
% answer with one of ours underneath it -- and suites here have bodies that
% do exactly that.
% A procedural font, and the current font once this has run. A suite that
% needs to show a character in order to reach something else -- a glyph's
% coverage, the colour the painting machinery resolves for one -- selects
% this rather than a face, because a build carrying no face library has no
% face to select and answers invalidfont to findfont. The glyph is a solid
% box, which is enough to be shown; suites about faces themselves ask
% `systemdict /NOFACES known` instead and leave their case out.
/testfont3 {                            % -  .  -
    /.testfont3dict 8 dict def
    .testfont3dict begin
        /FontType 3 def
        /FontMatrix [0.001 0 0 0.001 0 0] def
        /FontBBox [0 0 1000 1000] def
        /Encoding 256 array def
        0 1 255 { Encoding exch /.notdef put } for
        Encoding 65 /A put
        /CharProcs 2 dict def
        CharProcs /A { 700 0 0 0 700 700 setcachedevice
                       0 0 700 700 rectfill } put
        CharProcs /.notdef { 0 0 setcharwidth } put
        /BuildGlyph { exch /CharProcs get exch 2 copy known not
                      { pop /.notdef } if get exec } def
    end
    /.TestFont3 .testfont3dict definefont pop
    /.TestFont3 findfont 12 scalefont setfont
} bind def

/outcome {                              % { body }  .  /ok | /errorname
    //.testlib /gs get currentgstate pop
    count 1 sub //.testlib exch /base exch put
    stopped
    { //.testlib /gs get setgstate $error /errorname get }{ /ok } ifelse
    //.testlib exch /answer exch put
    //.testlib /unwind get exec
    //.testlib /answer get
} bind def

% (what) (subject)  note  -
% Report a failure a case detected itself, rather than one it raised. A
% case that checks something inside its own body has no boolean to hand
% back -- the body's value is whether it raised -- so it says so directly.
% Present because the suites that moved here already had it, spelled the
% same way in each: the strings are named question-then-subject, which is
% the other order from assert2, and both orders are kept rather than one
% suite's call sites rewritten to match the other's.
/note {                                 % (what) (subject)  .  -
    exch false 3 1 roll assert2
} bind def

% { body } (what)  works  -
% { body } (what) (subject)  works2  -
% The case must not raise.
/works {                                % { body } (what)  .  -
    //.testlib exch /what exch put
    outcome /ok eq //.testlib /what get assert
} bind def
/works2 {                               % { body } (what) (subject)  .  -
    //.testlib exch /subject exch put
    //.testlib exch /what exch put
    outcome /ok eq
    //.testlib /subject get //.testlib /what get assert2
} bind def

% { body } (what)  refuses  -
% { body } (what) (subject)  refuses2  -
% The case must raise, and these ask no more than that. That is the weaker
% question: a test satisfied by "some error" goes on passing when the
% operator starts refusing for the wrong reason, which is how a rangecheck
% quietly becomes a typecheck. Prefer the pair below, which name the error.
% These exist so a suite written before the distinction was drawn can be
% moved here unchanged rather than have its expectations rewritten too.
/refuses {                              % { body } (what)  .  -
    //.testlib exch /what exch put
    outcome /ok ne //.testlib /what get assert
} bind def
/refuses2 {                             % { body } (what) (subject)  .  -
    //.testlib exch /subject exch put
    //.testlib exch /what exch put
    outcome /ok ne
    //.testlib /subject get //.testlib /what get assert2
} bind def

% { body } /errorname (what)  raises  -
% { body } /errorname (what) (subject)  raises2  -
% The case must raise THAT error. This is the question worth asking, and
% the one a new case should use: the error name is part of the contract,
% and a test that does not check it cannot tell a correct refusal from a
% refusal for the wrong reason.
/raises {                               % { body } /errorname (what)  .  -
    //.testlib exch /what exch put
    //.testlib exch /want exch put
    outcome //.testlib /want get eq //.testlib /what get assert
} bind def
/raises2 {                              % { body } /errorname (what) (subject)  .  -
    //.testlib exch /subject exch put
    //.testlib exch /what exch put
    //.testlib exch /want exch put
    outcome //.testlib /want get eq
    //.testlib /subject get //.testlib /what get assert2
} bind def

% The dictionary-stack depth the suite starts at, read after the
% framework's own definitions so the framework is never counted as
% something a case leaked.
.testlib /depth countdictstack put

% One gstate object, reused by every case rather than allocated per case:
% the framework runs inside the suite's virtual memory and a suite that
% counts what it allocated should not be counting this.
.testlib /gs gstate put

% The depth a case starts at, so an unwind that has not been through outcome
% still has a number to pop back to rather than raising on a missing key.
.testlib /base 0 put
